n  was about three or four and he  absolutely loved that movie  um so i just i got to watch it many many  times  um but i remember at the time i mean i  quite like the movies there's lots  that's good about it  but what was really striking to me was  them walking around supermarkets  and seeing like cans of baked beans with  images from cars on it  and and wondering you know what is what  is the correlation between those two  things  how do the kids look at that and does it  make them think that the baked beans are  going to taste better because it's got  an image of the cars on it  i don't know um and i tried asking my  three-year-old but he was of course  useless  as as three-year-olds are um why  why do you want this thing right and  they're terrible co-investigators  um on the other hand they are super  interesting too because they kind of  point you towards things you might not  otherwise have paid any kind of  attention to  and so i became really fascinated about  how do these things function in a  marketing  sense and i started to think that really  what they do  is create territories and the reason i  think this is because  like if you try to um like if you watch  kids playing with with  toy versions of cars and so forth they  don't really kind of try to  replicate the movie in any kind of way  but they take  they take ideas and and  almost concepts from the movie which  they then use and recombine in their own  kinds of ways  so it seems to me that what's important  is that the film somehow creates a kind  of territory that that their desire can  occupy  it it's a kind of an imminent space in  which cars  can talk and cars can do crazy things  that they  they know that they don't do in reality  and this  enables them to occupy that space so  that the toys then become  kind of props that enable them to create  and occupy this space and that  seems to me that's how the advertising  works and i think also what's really  interesting is that we tend to  in my view not pay enough attention to  the notion of character  like it seems to me character is the  formation of a territory  we always think of character in plot  terms but if you talk  to people about films particularly film  franchises that they that they like  like in my family we're mad fans for the  whole  fast and furious franchise because  they're just so  super hilarious um and and whenever  we're driving  we always have this kind of um refrain  family  right family yeah exactly family but  also  what would dom do in this situation  i'm stuck in a traffic jam what would  dom do and i always use oh i'll just go  over the curb and i'll just go up on the  footpath and i'll get around these cars  and everybody like yep don would do it  um but that's that's extracting notions  of character and it becomes then  a kind of a territory that we can occupy  and it becomes a sort of  imminent space for the imagination and i  think to me  that's what's really fascinating i i and  i think that's why films work like  that's why we're interested in them  if you talk to kids and watch them  playing out there's you know various  versions of  you know frozen or whatever that's what  they take they don't really give a [ __ ]  about the storyline the storyline is  fairly immaterial to them  the storyline just creates the occasion  for these interesting characters  and what they do is take the characters  off on new journeys and  and new kinds of adventures for them for  themselves  and to me those are territories um and  and that's how i  try to think about them in the book  because as these are territories and we  should be interested in the production  of territories  and then ask well what kinds of um  investments of desire do we make into  these territories um  and you know that's to me how  contemporary popular culture works  yeah i i was as you were talking i was  thinking about the cinema books and  when when doula starts talking about the  the western and i was thinking and 